# Raspberry Pi 5 SD card image — A/B OTA layout  (optimised for 256 GB card)
#
# p1  /boot       256 MB   FAT32  kernel, DTBs, config.txt, cmdline.txt
# p2  /           8192 MB  ext4   rootfs-a  (active slot, populated at build time)
# p3  <none>      8192 MB  ext4   rootfs-b  (standby slot, written by SWUpdate)
# p4  /pst        1024 MB  ext4   persistent store (task 003)
# p5  /home       512 MB → grows to fill card on first boot via rpi5-home-init
#
# p4 is mounted at /pst by the packaged systemd unit pst.mount (LABEL=pst),
# NOT by a wic fstab entry: the plain .rootfs.ext4 that `make swu` packages
# carries no wic fstab entries, so a rootfs-packaged mount unit is the only
# mechanism that mounts identically in factory-flash and OTA images.
# /pst holds the istate-managed persistent store (system config, alarm
# state, collected logs, backups) and is NEVER written by SWUpdate.
#
# p5 is mounted at /home so user home directories (e.g. /home/rpi) persist
# across OTA updates.  On first boot, rpi5-home-init.service creates /home/rpi
# because the fresh empty partition hides the in-rootfs skeleton copy.
#
# ORDERING MATTERS: /pst must be declared BEFORE /home so /home remains the
# LAST partition — rpi5-home-init expands the /home partition to 100 % of
# the card with `parted resizepart <N> 100%`, which is only valid while /home
# is the final partition (the partition number is derived at runtime, so it
# works for p5 as well).
#
# GPT partition table (task 003): five data partitions do not fit in an
# msdos disklabel's four primaries.  The msdos fallback (extended container
# + logical partitions) would trap the LAST partition inside the container,
# so `parted resizepart <N> 100%` could never grow /home beyond the image
# footprint.  GPT has no such constraint, is required for RPi5 secure boot
# anyway, and is mandatory for CM5 NVMe boot (PCIe opt-in, cm5.yaml).
# PARTUUIDs become per-build UUIDs on GPT; the SWUpdate flow derives
# root=PARTUUID from the live device (post-install.sh) and slot switches
# are label-based (pre-install.sh), so nothing depends on cross-build
# PARTUUID stability.
#
# SWUpdate writes the new rootfs to the inactive slot (p2 or p3) and
# updates /boot/cmdline.txt to point root= at the new PARTUUID.
# On the next reboot the updated slot becomes active.

bootloader --ptable gpt --timeout=0

part /boot --source bootimg-partition --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=vfat --label boot --active --align 4096 --fixed-size 256M

part / --source rootfs --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label rootfs-a --align 4096 --fixed-size 8192M

part --source empty --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label rootfs-b --align 4096 --fixed-size 8192M

# p4: dedicated persistent store for istate-managed state (task 003).
# No mountpoint here on purpose — wic would write a fstab line, but the OTA
# rootfs (.rootfs.ext4 from `make swu`) does not carry wic fstab entries.
# pst.mount (acadia-pst-init recipe) mounts it by LABEL and skips itself
# silently on devices still using the old 4-partition layout.
#
# NOTE on --source: `--source empty` (the EmptyPartitionPlugin) creates an
# UNFORMATTED partition (pure zeros, no mkfs).  To get an mkfs'd empty ext4
# volume, omit --source so partition.py takes its empty-partition branch
# (prepare_empty_partition_ext).  rootfs-b (p3) keeps --source empty
# deliberately: SWUpdate streams the complete rootfs image onto it, so an
# unformatted target is exactly what it wants.
part --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label pst --align 4096 --fixed-size 1024M

# p5 is created at 512 MB here so the .wic image stays small.
# rpi5-home-init.service expands it to fill the actual card on first boot.
part /home --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label home --align 4096 --fixed-size 512M
